# EXPERIMENT 5 – OSPF ROUTING CONFIGURATION

**Important:** The uploaded Experiment 5 PDF has a title/aim mismatch on its first page. The procedure from the following pages describes OSPF routing using three routers. The steps below follow that OSPF procedure.

## 1. Aim
To configure Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) routing between three routers and enable communication between different LAN networks.

## 2. Devices Required
- 3 Routers (R0, R1 and R2)
- 3 Switches
- 3 PCs
- Serial DCE/DTE cables
- Copper Straight-Through cables

## 3. Network Design

Create three LAN networks:

- LAN 1: `192.168.1.0/24`
- LAN 2: `192.168.2.0/24`
- LAN 3: `192.168.3.0/24`

Connect each PC to its switch and each switch to the FastEthernet 0/0 interface of its router.

Connect the three routers in a triangle using serial connections:

- R0 S0/0 ↔ R1 S0/0
- R0 S0/1 ↔ R2 S0/0
- R1 S0/1 ↔ R2 S0/1

## 4. PC IP Address Configuration

| Device | IP Address | Gateway |
|---|---|---|
| PC1 | 192.168.1.2 | 192.168.1.1 |
| PC2 | 192.168.2.2 | 192.168.2.1 |
| PC3 | 192.168.3.2 | 192.168.3.1 |

Subnet mask: `255.255.255.0`

## 5. Router Interface IP Configuration

### R0

```text
enable
configure terminal

interface fastethernet 0/0
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit

interface serial 0/0
ip address 10.0.0.1 255.255.255.252
clock rate 64000
no shutdown
exit

interface serial 0/1
ip address 10.0.0.5 255.255.255.252
clock rate 64000
no shutdown
exit

end
```

### R1

```text
enable
configure terminal

interface fastethernet 0/0
ip address 192.168.2.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit

interface serial 0/0
ip address 10.0.0.2 255.255.255.252
no shutdown
exit

interface serial 0/1
ip address 10.0.0.9 255.255.255.252
clock rate 64000
no shutdown
exit

end
```

### R2

```text
enable
configure terminal

interface fastethernet 0/0
ip address 192.168.3.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit

interface serial 0/0
ip address 10.0.0.6 255.255.255.252
no shutdown
exit

interface serial 0/1
ip address 10.0.0.10 255.255.255.252
no shutdown
exit

end
```

**Clock rate note:** Set `clock rate 64000` only on the actual DCE ends. Check the cable end using the router's interface information if necessary. The manual lists the clock rate on R0's serial interfaces and R1's S0/1; verify the DCE ends in your actual topology.

## 6. OSPF Configuration

OSPF is configured on all three routers.

### R0

```text
enable
configure terminal

router ospf 2
router-id 1.1.1.1
network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.0.0.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
network 10.0.0.4 0.0.0.3 area 0

end
```

### R1

```text
enable
configure terminal

router ospf 2
router-id 2.2.2.2
network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.0.0.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
network 10.0.0.8 0.0.0.3 area 0

end
```

### R2

```text
enable
configure terminal

router ospf 2
router-id 3.3.3.3
network 192.168.3.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.0.0.4 0.0.0.3 area 0
network 10.0.0.8 0.0.0.3 area 0

end
```

## 7. Verify OSPF

On each router, enter:

```text
show ip route
show ip protocols
show ip interface brief
```

In the routing table:
- `C` = Connected route.
- `O` = OSPF-learned route.

## 8. Testing

From PC1:

```text
ping 192.168.2.2
ping 192.168.3.2
```

From PC2:

```text
ping 192.168.3.2
```

The pings should succeed when all router interfaces and OSPF adjacencies are working.

Save the configurations:

```text
copy running-config startup-config
```

## 9. Expected Result

OSPF routing is successfully configured between three routers, allowing all three LAN networks to communicate automatically.

