# EXPERIMENT 6 – VLAN CONFIGURATION AND INTER-VLAN ROUTING

## 1. Aim
To configure VLANs on a switch, test communication within the same VLAN, and enable communication between different VLANs using Router-on-a-Stick.

## 2. Devices Required
- 1 Router
- 1 Layer 2 Switch (2960)
- 4 PCs
- Copper Straight-Through cables

## 3. Network Design

Create two VLANs:

- VLAN 10 – Student
- VLAN 20 – Faculty

Connect:
- PC-A to Switch Fa0/1
- PC-B to Switch Fa0/2
- PC-C to Switch Fa0/3
- PC-D to Switch Fa0/4

Use Switch Fa0/24 to connect to the router's GigabitEthernet 0/0 interface, as specified in the main topology instructions.

## 4. IP Address Configuration

| Device | VLAN | IP Address | Gateway |
|---|---|---|---|
| PC-A | 10 | 192.168.1.2 | 192.168.1.1 |
| PC-B | 10 | 192.168.1.3 | 192.168.1.1 |
| PC-C | 20 | 172.16.1.2 | 172.16.1.1 |
| PC-D | 20 | 172.16.1.3 | 172.16.1.1 |

Subnet mask for all PCs: `255.255.255.0`

## 5. Switch VLAN Configuration

Open Switch → CLI.

```text
enable
configure terminal

vlan 10
name vlan student
exit

vlan 20
name vlan faculty
exit

interface range fastethernet 0/1 - 2
switchport mode access
switchport access vlan 10
exit

interface range fastethernet 0/3 - 4
switchport mode access
switchport access vlan 20
exit

end
```

Verify:

```text
show vlan brief
```

You should see:
- Fa0/1 and Fa0/2 in VLAN 10.
- Fa0/3 and Fa0/4 in VLAN 20.

## 6. Test Intra-VLAN Communication

From PC-A:

```text
ping 192.168.1.3
```

From PC-C:

```text
ping 172.16.1.3
```

Both should succeed.

Now test communication between different VLANs:

From PC-A:

```text
ping 172.16.1.2
```

This should fail before inter-VLAN routing is configured.

## 7. Configure the Trunk Port

The trunk port connects the switch to the router.

On the switch CLI:

```text
enable
configure terminal

interface fastethernet 0/24
switchport mode trunk
switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20

end
```

Note: Use the actual switch port connected to the router. If your topology uses another port, replace Fa0/24 with that port.

## 8. Router-on-a-Stick Configuration

Open Router → CLI.

The router physical interface should not have an IP address. Configure the subinterfaces instead.

```text
enable
configure terminal

interface gigabitethernet 0/0
no ip address
no shutdown
exit

interface gigabitethernet 0/0.10
encapsulation dot1Q 10
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
exit

interface gigabitethernet 0/0.20
encapsulation dot1Q 20
ip address 172.16.1.1 255.255.255.0
exit

end
write memory
```

If your router uses FastEthernet 0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet 0/0, use the actual connected physical interface name consistently for both subinterfaces.

## 9. Verify Configuration

On the switch:

```text
show vlan brief
show interfaces trunk
```

On the router:

```text
show ip interface brief
```

## 10. Test Inter-VLAN Communication

From PC-A:

```text
ping 172.16.1.2
```

From PC-C:

```text
ping 192.168.1.2
```

Both should now succeed.

## 11. Expected Result

VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 are successfully created. Devices within the same VLAN communicate through the switch, and devices in different VLANs communicate through Router-on-a-Stick.

